# tests/hello, as an ENTRY (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3): evaluating this
# directory yields the test's ARG TREE, which dev/run-test then runs.
#
# A WORKER TEST. `std/hello` is a worker, and every claim here is about what it
# reports; the client was the way its result got onto a terminal, which a
# `then` stage reads just as well.
#
# The one claim that LOOKED client-shaped — `--base:@=outer/tool` resolving
# through an ANCESTOR expression — is not: `eval-path-then` performs the SAME
# walk (crate caos-eval) on a server thread, which is precisely what
# tests/eval-then pins down. Resolution is not a client property; BLOCKING on
# it is, and blocking was never the subject.
#
# `--hello:@=DEEP-DEPS/hello` hands this worker the BUILT image; the UNBUILT
# entry is reached through `/cas/args/in/DEEP-DEPS/hello`, which is the test's
# own deepened tree.
curry --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/bash --worker1:@=worker.sh --hello:@=DEEP-DEPS/hello --lift:@=lift.sh
